d F4, then need a pair from W7 or F4. That could work. Pung of T3 gives \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\"? No, T3 is simple, not terminal/honor. So no extra points.  \n\nGiven I need points, discarding F4 seems good because it opens \"All Simples\" and \"No Honors\", and I already have \"Two Terminal Chows\" (1 point). But as calculated, that's only 6-7 points. I might need more. Perhaps I should keep F4 and try for \"Half Flush\"? Half Flush requires only one suit and honors. My melds: two Bamboo chows, one Dots chow. If I can convert the Dots chow into a Bamboo chow, then all melds would be Bamboo plus honors. But I have a Dots chow exposed, so cannot change. So Half Flush is impossible because I already have a Dots meld. So discard F4 is better.  \n\nBut wait, maybe I can aim for \"All Chows\" + \"All Simples\" + \"Two Terminal Chows\" + \"Self Drawn\" + \"Edge Wait\" or \"Closed Wait\". That might reach 8 if I get two of the 1-point patterns. For example, if my winning tile is T3 to complete the pair, that's \"Single Wait\" (1 point) if it's the only tile. But \"Single Wait\" requires that the winning tile is the only tile to complete the pair. If I have pair T3 and need T3 to win, that's single wait. Also, if I win by self-draw, that's another 1 point. So total: All Chows (2) + All Simples (2) + No Honors (1) + Two Terminal Chows (1) + Single Wait (1) + Self Drawn (1) = 8 points. That works! So if I discard F4, and then draw T5 to complete Chow T3-T4-T5, and then draw another T3 to complete the pair (or draw F4 for pair but that would break No Honors), I can win with 8 points. But I need to ensure the wait is single wait. If I have pair T3 and need T3, that's single wait. If I have pair T3 and also could win with another tile? For example, if I have Chow T3-T4-T5 and pair T3, then my hand is complete except for pair. So the only winning tile is T3 to complete the pair. So yes, single wait. So that's plausible.  \n\nNow, if I discard T3, I lose the ready pair, making it harder to complete a pair. Discarding W7 or T4 also keeps the pair T3, but keeps F4 which costs All Simples and No Honors. So discarding F4 seems best.  \n\nCheck unshown tiles: T5 has 2 left, T3 has 2 left. So chances to draw T5 then T3 are decent. Also, I could draw T3 first to have pung T3, then need Chow and pair? Actually, if I draw T3, I'd have three T3, which is a pung. Then my hand: pung T3, plus W7, T4, F4. Then I need a Chow and a pair. Chow could be T4 with T5 and T6? T5 (2 left), T6 (1 left). Or pair could be W7 or F4. But then I'd have a pung, so not All Chows. That reduces points. So I'd rather not pung T3 if possible, to keep All Chows. So I should avoid drawing a third T3. But if I discard F4, I have pair T3, so drawing a third T3 would give me three T3, which I could choose not to declare pung (since it's concealed, I can keep it as pair + extra T3? But for winning hand, I need four melds and a pair. If I have three T3, that can be a meld (pung) or part of a chow? No, three identical tiles are a pung, not a chow. So if I get three T3, I would have to use them as a pung, breaking All Chows. So to preserve All Chows, I need to avoid having three of a kind. So I should hope to draw T5 for Chow, and not draw T3. But if I draw T3, I could discard it? But then I'd lose the pair. So maybe I should keep the pair T3 and discard the extra if I draw a third T3? But then I'd have to discard something else, possibly breaking the hand. This is tricky.  \n\nGiven the game state, discarding F4 seems the most promising to aim for a winning hand with sufficient points.  \n\nReason: Discarding F4 allows the hand to potentially achieve \"All Chows\" (2), \"All Simples\" (2), \"No Honors\" (1), \"Two Terminal Chows\" (1), and with \"Single Wait\" (1) and \"Self Drawn\" (1), reaching 8 points. Keeping F4 would prevent \"All Simples\" and \"No Honors\", making it harder to reach 8 points. The pair of T3 is valuable as a ready pair, and drawing T5 (2 left) completes